    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive configuration, headlined by **Trea Turner** (77.53) and **Kyle Schwarber** (76.68). Both benefit significantly from a favorable park factor and a moderate matchup against Bradish, despite the 41% rain risk clouding their availability. **Christian Yelich** (75.84) also stands out as an elite play; his matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a provides a strong platoon advantage, and the combination of an elite catcher\u2019s pop time and the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery keeps his stolen base potential high, which remains a critical KPI for our roster construction. **Yordan Alvarez** (73.79) rounds out the top tier, offering the highest baseline power projection of the day, effectively neutralizing the challenge posed by Rocker.\n\nMid-tier value is anchored by **Junior Caminero** (65.48) and **Lars Nootbaar** (65.16), both of whom see their utility scores buoyed by significant SP skill adjustments. **Lars Nootbaar** in particular is a high-upside play today, leveraging an 11% platoon edge and a favorable park factor against Scherzer. While **Dillon Dingler** (60.91) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (61.91) carry minor situational risks\u2014including wind resistance for **Pete Crow-Armstrong**\u2014their dynamic platoon advantages and top-of-the-order positioning make them essential starts.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Chase Meidroth**, who finished just outside the active threshold with a 36.63 score. While he possesses the talent to contribute, the negative park adjustments and a suboptimal batting order position left him well below our 40.0 efficiency floor. Consequently, we are opting to bench him to preserve our limited game caps for more favorable deployments. Management remains vigilant regarding the rain risk for **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber**; should the weather force a late-scratch, we have positioned our bench to manage the resulting cap attrition carefully.",
